      note := "For every validated calculus, an open-search machine separates pending goals from an ordered ledger of discharged premise positions. Rule steps retain their rule instances and assumption steps retain their positions. Every open derivation constructs a Type-valued complete route; every complete route reconstructs an open derivation, and the reconstructed proof's leaf-position list equals the final ledger. Reconstructing a proof-generated route recovers that exact proof tree even with trailing-goal continuations, and the dependent proof-to-route-and-ledger receipt is injective. Every completed route is exactly regenerated by running its reconstructed proof: the actual event trace identifies the entire dependent route from its starting state. Thus all completed route-and-ledger receipts are equivalent to authored open proofs, without a canonicality restriction. This concerns the pending-first semantic machine, not additional scheduling or failed-search histories of another implementation. Fixed-ledger inhabitation is equivalent in both directions. Substituting a route receipt reconstructs its proof and runs the substituted proof; it computes the ordered flat-map ledger, reconstructs exactly to the substituted proof, commutes with proof-generated routes, and composes. Identity substitution preserves every completed route receipt exactly. Proof substitution substitutes ordered premise-use lists by flatMap. A derivation-valued interpretation preserves every open proof's ordered ledger if and only if each admitted source rule template uses all premise positions exactly once in order; the converse tests the rule against the identity premise environment. This condition holds for identity and composition, and a supplied source proof constructively yields a target search route ending at that same ledger. The open-search machine is also a proof-relevant GSLT: exact-ledger certificates exist precisely when its closure OSLF diamond reaches the completed state. Ordered-linear interpretation preserves this exact-completion closure diamond forward; a concrete target-only A-to-B rule proves that the converse fails. No preservation of arbitrary modal predicates is claimed. Duplicate equal judgments yield distinct accepted ledger targets, while a concrete source proof needs two primitive events and cannot finish by one primitive modal step. The two-step target interpretation satisfies ordered linearity and carries the concrete source closure receipt forward; a second valid implementation duplicates one premise and fails it. The head-only proof-search GSLT cannot resume behind an unresolved assumption. The generic map from a Prop-valued Step to a Type-valued route uses choice; a separate certificate-fed route construction is constructive. This is not an executable proof-search decision procedure, resource preservation for arbitrary interpretations, or Prime's authored DTT." }
