      note := "The live collection applier leaves its rest unresolved when the bound value is not a same-kind rest-free collection, while still reporting a reduct; the three witnessed cases distinguish this from successful splicing. The two-sort example proves that its declared sequence metavariable can instead be instantiated for every sequence value without a shape test. It does not prove general variable elimination: minSig still stores restOp names as base operators, and erase_of_splice_with_rest and erased_splice_with_base_rest_is_not_ground show that such a name survives typed instantiation as an unresolved MeTTa rest. A genuine closed-run input has a ground erased result. The sort-indexed erasure is proved for this minimal signature, not for the canonical LanguageDef or its executable matcher. Separately, the authored sorting audit identifies a disagreement: the current decision procedure rejects collection rests, while the declarative collection rules do not consult their declaration. Its checked HE/Pure/rho/Metamath rows diagnose that restricted sorting layer; they do not establish complete rule validation or a shared sequence-sort semantics. Integrating declared element/sequence metavariables, explicit occurrence substitutions and premise-output origins into one LanguageDef-derived rule algebra remains open. The inward capture repair and its depth-aligned conservativity theorem do not close these obligations."
    }
  , { area := "Syntax"
      title := "Chapter 17: splittings, locations as fibres, and the sparseness of available events"
      status := .inProgress
      codeRef := "Mettapedia/OSLF/Syntax/ContextualSplitting.lean: Splitting / plug / shape_does_not_locate / positionOfFibre / fibreOfPosition / fibreOfPosition_plug / positionOfFibre_fibreOfPosition / fibreOfPosition_positionOfFibre / Fire / residual / fire_gives_a_step / fire_source; Mettapedia/OSLF/Syntax/FireSparseness.lean: FireSparse.state / FireSparse.liveCut / FireSparse.deadCut / FireSparse.same_state / FireSparse.liveCut_fires / FireSparse.isOut / FireSparse.rootStep_source_is_out / FireSparse.nilS_is_not_out / FireSparse.deadCut_is_inert / FireSparse.fire_is_sparse / FireSparse.liveCut_is_a_direction; Finding Mind draft 26 sections 17.2 to 17.5"
      note := "The source separates two things that are easy to conflate. A one-hole context is a shape: it says what the surroundings look like, and the same shape sits around indefinitely many subterms of indefinitely many terms. What locates is the pair. Definition 17.2 makes a splitting of a term the pair of a context and a subterm, with the plug map sending a splitting to the term it reconstructs and forgetting where the cut was, and a location in a term a point of the fibre of that map over it. Both halves are built here, and the source's own argument that a context does not locate is proved: one shape, two subterms, two different terms. The identification of locations with the fibre is then proved in both directions, with both round trips, so the position structure is not merely analogous to a fibre but is one - which is also why a position carries a plugging equation rather than a path. Definition 17.3's bundle of available events is built on top: a splitting is an event when its subterm side exposes a redex of the rule, the context side being where that rewrite is to fire, and the residual is the term the state becomes with the context side untouched. An available event is proved to be a direction the state can move in, which is the discrete tangent reading the source gives it. The sparseness claim of 17.4 is proved in its sharpest available form: one state, two cuts that reconstruct it, one an available event and the other inert, so the bundle is proper over a single fibre and sparseness is a property of cuts rather than of states. That the second cut is inert is proved structurally rather than by inspection - every source of a root step is headed by the rule's own head constructor, and the payload is not. Why this row is open: contextual splitting is built but its laws are not - composition of cuts, the relation between a cut and the enumeration of positions, and the residual's behaviour under the equational theory; the chapter's spatial-temporal correspondence is stated as the tangent reading of a single event rather than of paths; and chapter 16's labelled transitions, which 17 is supposed to sit over, are untouched here."
    }
