lFragment.pow_add / ParallelFragment.rep_add / ParallelFragment.countOut_pow / ParallelFragment.countOut_rep / ParallelFragment.to_rep_out / ParallelFragment.to_rep_bounded / ParallelFragment.to_rep / ParallelFragment.eq_of_countOut_eq / ParallelFragment.SupportedBy / ParallelFragment.supportedBy_congruent / ParallelFragment.unique_decomposition_of_disjoint_support / ParallelFragment.phi_is_not_support_closed / ParallelFragment.corrected_hypothesis_excludes_the_counterexample; Finding Mind draft 26 section 18.2, Proposition 18.1 and its uses in Proposition 18.2, Corollary 18.1 and Table 18.1"
      note := "The AC1 development proves a genuine negative result: disjoint equation-invariant predicates alone do not guarantee a unique split of their parallel composition. It does not refute the full source Proposition 18.1. FM also requires grade-zero separation, defined by shared free-name support; the current witness has no theorem discharging that condition for the two scope extensions. An empty rewrite relation is not evidence of this separate grading condition. The source proposition and its dependent decidability argument therefore remain open. Independently, the repaired three-prime AC1 example proves uniqueness under disjoint prime support, using output multiplicities as a complete invariant. The replication-unfolding example shows why that invariant is not generic to arbitrary equation theories. The source description-length corollary remains separately open; neither the weaker counterexample nor the repaired support theorem establishes its cost assumptions."
    }
