  , { area := "Syntax"
      title := "The generated modalities inside the observational logic, and the layer the observations cannot see"
      status := .inProgress
      codeRef := "Mettapedia/OSLF/Syntax/ModalityAndObservation.lean: Presentation.act_of_rootStep / Presentation.sat_dia_of_poss / Presentation.exists_sat_dia_of_stepsFromPosition / Presentation.exists_sat_dia_of_relyPossibly / ParallelFragment.pmetas / ParallelFragment.pSig / ParallelFragment.dropRight / ParallelFragment.dropping / ParallelFragment.dropInstance / ParallelFragment.dropRight_rootStep / ParallelFragment.rightCtxt / ParallelFragment.holeCount_rightCtxt / ParallelFragment.contextual_step / ParallelFragment.sat_dia_contextual / ParallelFragment.headIsPar / ParallelFragment.leftParand / ParallelFragment.not_poss_contextual / ParallelFragment.poss_strictly_refines_dia / truePreds / satArgs_truePreds / HeadedBy / structural_truePreds_iff_headedBy / no_invariant_predicate_is_headedBy / ParallelFragment.isPar / ParallelFragment.isPar_iff_headedBy / ParallelFragment.isPar_parT / ParallelFragment.not_isPar_u / ParallelFragment.structural_not_equation_invariant / ParallelFragment.no_atom_is_isPar"
      note := "Two modal layers sit over the same presentation, and leaving them unrelated would be a defect of the development rather than of the source. The generated logic's possibility is a root-level statement indexed by a rule; the observational logic's diamond is a statement about the transition system, which closes the rule under contexts and under the equations. The relation is settled here, and it is a strict refinement rather than a coincidence. A root step at a rule is a transition at that rule's label; the generated possibility therefore inhabits the corresponding diamond, and so, after passing through the step the modality delivers, do the modality at a chosen position and the rely-indexed modality - the latter two yielding a state that can move rather than a modal claim about their own subject, since their subject lives at the position's carrier and not at the rule's sort, which is the type-theoretic content of placing the modality at the carrier. The refinement is strict, and the witness is exhibited rather than asserted: a presentation with a single rule that drops the right parand of a composition, a state whose transition is available only inside a context, and the proof that the rule cannot produce that state at the root because a root step of this rule returns the left parand. So inhabiting a generated modality is a stronger claim than satisfying the corresponding diamond - it names the rule and it names the redex, where the diamond asserts only that the state can move. That the two differ is the expected answer: were they to agree, the contextual closure would have nothing to do and the chapter on labelled transitions would add nothing to the chapter on the generator. The second half separates the layers in the other direction. The structural type formers generated at the term formers are predicates on terms and read a term's head; observations of a transition system are predicates on classes and cannot. As soon as the equations identify terms with different heads these are not comparable, and that is proved: the unit law identifies a composition with its own left parand while the structural former at the composition separates them, so no atom of the generated transition system agrees with that former. Why this row is open: the refinement is proved in one direction only, with no characterisation of when a diamond does come from a generated modality, which is where the contextual splitting of the chapter on space would have to be used; and the modality layer has no Hennessy-Milner-style adequacy of its own."
    }
  , { area := "Syntax"
      title := "Every transition is a located event, and every located event is a generated modality"
      status := .inProgress
      codeRef := "Mettapedia/OSLF/Syntax/TransitionsAreEvents.lean: StepAt / step_of_stepAt / stepAt_of_rootStep / stepAt_congr / stepAt_of_fire_in_context / fire_of_rootStep / step_iff_cut / exists_fire_of_step / exists_stepsFromPosition_of_rootStep / exists_stepsFromPosition_of_fire / Presentation.act_is_a_located_modality / Presentation.act_of_fire / ParallelFragment.contextual_step_is_located / ParallelFragment.contextual_step_gives_a_modality"
      note := "Three layers describe the same thing from three sides. The labelled transition system says a state can move; the splitting of a term into a shape and a subterm says where a move would happen; the generated modality says which rule moves and what it needs at the chosen position. Until they are related each is a separate development that happens to share a signature, and the generated modality had only been shown to refine the diamond in one direction. Here the refinement is closed into a characterisation, and the thing that closes it is the splitting. A step of the generated relation is exactly a cut at which the rule fires, proved as an equivalence rather than a construction, so the relation is the bundle of available events projected to its endpoints and every state that can move does so at an available event whose reconstruction is that state. The root step inside such an event carries an instance, and that instance names a redex at the position's carrier, so the event hands back the generated modality there with the root step's own reduct as its target. At the level of the transition system the chain runs end to end: from a labelled transition one recovers the cut where it happened, the reduct firing produces, the available event at that cut, and the generated modality at the position, with the equations appearing only at the two ends, which is where the transition system put them; and conversely an available event at a linear cut is a transition at that rule's label. This is what makes the splitting load-bearing rather than decorative, since nothing else in the development carries a transition back to a located modality. Two controls record that the chain is inhabited on a rule that fires only under a context. Events also compose. An available event is a cut at which the rule fires, two cuts compose, and so an event inside a context is a step of the enclosing term, at the composite cut - which is the congruence property that makes the generated relation an operational semantics rather than a set of pairs. Stating it needs the cut to be structurally linear, since in the variable representation a composite of two linear contexts is linear only by a theorem about how occurrence counts multiply through a substitution, while in the structural representation the composite is linear because its type says so; the relation is therefore presented once more with its cut structural, proved to refine the relation as given, and congruence is then immediate. The structural presentation of the relation coincides with the given one, since a context with exactly one occurrence of its hole is proved to be the reading of a structural context and conversely. Why this row is open: the characterisation recovers a modality from a transition but does not say which modality, since the redex it names is the one the instance chose and no uniqueness is claimed for it; the equations are carried along the two ends rather than through the splitting, so there is no notion yet of a cut taken modulo the equational theory; and the composition of cuts, which is what would make the located events a category rather than a set, is still not built."
    }
  , { area := "Syntax"
      title := "Contexts compose: two representations of a one-hole context and the translation between them"
      status := .inProgress
      codeRef := "Mettapedia/OSLF/Syntax/ContextCategory.lean: ContextCat.Hole / ContextCat.holeSub / ContextCat.comp / ContextCat.inst_comp / ContextCat.comp_hole / ContextCat.hole_comp / ContextCat.comp_assoc / ContextCat.contextCategory / ContextCat.act / ContextCat.act_id / ContextCat.act_comp / ContextCat.Splitting.refine / ContextCat.plug_refine / ContextCat.refine_trivial; Mettapedia/OSLF/Syntax/BindingSignature.lean: liftSub_weakenVar / sameVar_self / liftRen_weakenVar / sameVar_weakenVar_liftRen_reflect / countVar_rename_of_reflect / countVarArgs_rename_of_reflect; Mettapedia/OSLF/Syntax/Strengthening.lean: Strengthener / Strengthener.liftUnStep / Strengthener.liftS / Strengthener.ofWeaken / strengthenT / strengthenA / rename_strengthenT / renameArgs_strengthenA / Strengthener.liftS_un_eq_none / strengthenT_isSome / strengthenA_isSome / unweaken / exists_unweaken / exists_unweaken_iff / strengthenT_rename / strengthenA_renameArgs / rename_injective / renameArgs_injective; Mettapedia/OSLF/Syntax/LinearContexts.lean: LinCtx.unexch / LinCtx.unexch_exch / LinCtx.exch_unexch / LinCtx.exchStrengthener / LinCtx.toTerm_injective / LinCtx.toArgs_injective / LinCtx.toTerm_hom_injective / LinCtx.exists_linCtx / LinCtx.exists_linArgs / LinCtx.exists_linCtx_of_holeCount / LinCtx.toTerm_surjective_onto_linear / LinCtx / LinArgs / LinCtx.shift / LinCtx.exch / LinCtx.exch_zero / LinCtx.sameVar_exch / LinCtx.renameLin / LinCtx.renameLinArgs / LinCtx.renameLin_renameLin / LinCtx.renameLinArgs_renameLinArgs / LinCtx.under / LinCtx.renameLin_comp / LinCtx.renameLinArgs_compArgs / LinCtx.liftRen_shift_commute / LinCtx.toTerm_renameLin / LinCtx.toArgs_renameLinArgs / LinCtx.DeepHole.sig / LinCtx.DeepHole.underBinder / LinCtx.DeepHole.underBinder_linear / LinCtx.DeepHole.underBinder_not_shallow / LinCtx.toTerm / LinCtx.toArgs / LinCtx.countVar_toTerm / LinCtx.countVarArgs_toArgs / LinCtx.holeCount_toTerm / LinCtx.comp / LinCtx.compArgs / LinCtx.comp_hole / LinCtx.compArgs_hole / LinCtx.hole_comp / LinCtx.comp_assoc / LinCtx.compArgs_assoc / LinCtx.holeCount_comp / LinCtx.toTerm_comp / LinCtx.toArgs_compArgs / LinCtx.Obj / LinCtx.linearContextCategory / LinCtx.toTerm_id / LinCtx.toTerm_comp_hom / LinCtx.no_vacuous_linCtx / LinCtx.variable_representation_admits_vacuous; Finding Mind draft 26 section 17.3"
      note := "The splitting development said where a rewrite happens but had no way to say that one place sits inside another, so the cuts were a set of points rather than a structure. Composition supplies that, and read correctly a one-hole context already is a morphism, from the hole it has to the sort it makes. That reading is exact rather than suggestive: the bare hole is an identity on both sides, plugging one context into another's hole is composition, associativity holds on the nose, and the three laws are the unit and associativity of substitution read on contexts instead of on substitutions, so they are not new work but the same phenomenon a second time. Plugging a term into the hole is then the action of that category on terms, and the action is functorial - plugging into a composite is plugging twice, which is the statement that nesting loses and duplicates nothing at a seam. Refining a splitting by cutting its subterm side again is composition in that category, and refining does not change the state the splitting reconstructs, which is what makes a finer place still a place in the same thing. There is a second representation, and the two trade off rather than one being better. A context written as a term with a distinguished free variable makes the category laws free and makes linearity a side condition: nothing in the type stops the hole occurring twice or not at all, so every use carries a hypothesis and composing two cuts would need a theorem about how occurrence counts multiply through a substitution. A context written as an inductive shape with a hole constructor makes linearity a fact about the type, and then the law that was hard in the first representation - linear cuts compose to a linear cut - is immediate, with nothing to prove about counts. Both are built, both are categories, and the translation from the second to the first is proved to carry identities to identities and composition to nesting, so no result about either has to be reproved for the other. The two representations are then shown to be genuinely different rather than notational variants: the structural one admits no context that ignores its hole, and the variable one does, which is exactly what the side condition is for. The hole may sit under binders, which the class the position enumerator covers does not allow, and a witness on the smallest signature that has a binder exhibits the difference rather than asserting it. That generality costs one lemma, stated for an arbitrary renaming rather than at the point of use: reading such a context as a term carries the hole past the binders it sits under, which is an exchange, and an exchange neither creates nor destroys occurrences of the hole only because a renaming that reflects sameness preserves occurrence counts exactly. Composition then weakens the context being plugged in, into whatever binders the hole sits under, so renaming of structural contexts is developed alongside and proved to commute with composition and with the translation. The translation is then proved to identify the two: it is injective, and every context of the variable representation whose hole occurs exactly once is the reading of a structural one, so the side condition and the constructor are two descriptions of one thing rather than two notions related by a map. Faithfulness is proved by inverting the exchange that carries a hole past a binder prefix rather than by taking a constructor of an inductive family apart, which is what makes it go through: the inverse exchange is written down, both round trips are proved, and renaming along it is injective because it has a partial inverse. Fullness recurses on a structural measure rather than on the term, since the argument carrying the hole is examined through that exchange and a renamed subterm is not a subterm; renaming leaves the measure unchanged, which is what licenses the step, and the arguments away from the hole are moved out of its scope by strengthening. Why this row is open: the available events of the splitting development are not yet shown to be closed under refinement, which is what would make the located events themselves a category rather than a family over one."
    }
  , { area := "Syntax"
      title := "Terms of a binding signature are a multisorted clone, and the two categories of contexts agree"
      status := .done
      codeRef := "Mettapedia/OSLF/Syntax/TermClone.lean: varIdx / get_varIdx / varOfIdx / varIdx_varOfIdx / substVar / substVar_varOfIdx / substVar_of_sub / substVar_bind / termClone / termClone_Hom / termClone_substitute / envEquivSub / envEquivSub_id / envEquivSub_comp; Mettapedia/GSLT/LanguageDef/MultiSortedClone.lean"
      note := "The unit and associativity of substitution were proved for their own sake and then read again as the laws of a category of contexts. They are also exactly the laws of a multisorted clone, which this project already had: an abstract operation with an ordered context of input sorts and one output sort, positional variables, and simultaneous substitution over a shared context. Rather than let the two developments sit beside each other, the terms of a binding signature are made an instance of the existing structure, so everything proved about clones applies to them and the abstract laws are discharged by the concrete ones rather than restated. The one thing to reconcile is how a variable is named. A clone indexes its inputs positionally, by a position into the context together with the sort that position carries; this development indexes them by a typed de Bruijn index, which is the same information arranged so that the sort is a parameter rather than a projection. The translation is arranged so that no transport appears anywhere: the recursion peels the context and the position together, so each clause's sort equation holds by reduction rather than by a cast, which is what would otherwise make the instance unusable. The three clone laws are then the unit of substitution, its associativity, and the variable clause of substitution. Both presentations of the category of contexts are shown to agree rather than one being retired: the clone's morphisms are positional environments and this development's are typed substitutions, the hom-sets are in bijection, and the bijection carries identities to identities and composition to composition. So neither is a duplicate of the other - one is the instance of the abstract structure and the other is the form the rest of the development uses - and a result proved in either is available in both."
    }
  , { area := "Framework"
      title := "Finitary HML adequacy cannot hold uniformly without additional hypotheses"
      status := .done
      codeRef := "Mettapedia/GSLT/Logic/ImageFinitenessNecessary.lean: ImageFinitenessNecessary.St / ImageFinitenessNecessary.Step / ImageFinitenessNecessary.sys / ImageFinitenessNecessary.hm / ImageFinitenessNecessary.depth / ImageFinitenessNecessary.agree / ImageFinitenessNecessary.logically_equivalent / ImageFinitenessNecessary.Runs / ImageFinitenessNecessary.runs_endless / ImageFinitenessNecessary.not_runs_chain / ImageFinitenessNecessary.runs_of_bisim / ImageFinitenessNecessary.not_bisimilar / ImageFinitenessNecessary.chain_injective / ImageFinitenessNecessary.not_imageFinite / ImageFinitenessNecessary.adequacy_needs_image_finiteness / ImageFinitenessNecessary.bisimilar_refl_here; Mettapedia/GSLT/Logic/HennessyMilnerAdequacy.lean: System.ImageFiniteModulo; Finding Mind draft 26 Theorem 16.1"
      note := "The genuine infinite-branching counterexample has two logically equivalent states that are not bisimilar, and its successor classes cannot be covered by a finite set. Thus the uniform converse cannot simply drop image-finiteness without another hypothesis or a stronger logic. Image-finiteness is sufficient for the existing generic finitary correspondence; it is not necessary in every individual adequate system. The example is a generic one-label transition system. It does not establish that the source's full minimal-context and higher-order-label hypotheses hold, so it is not a refutation of the full source theorem."
    }
  , { area := "MeTTaIL"
